<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance comparison on Tennessee Eastman Process dataset. Best scores for each metric are highlighted in <span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Reported DMCD values ‣ 5 Results ‣ DMCD: Semantic-Statistical Framework for Causal Discovery" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates the results of applying DMCD to the Tennessee Eastman Process benchmark alongside ten established causal discovery methods. While NOTEARS achieves the lowest SHD and PC obtains the best FDR and Precision, DMCD outperforms all competitors in TPR, FPR, Recall, and F1 score. These results indicate a favorable balance between discovery capability and false positive control, which is particularly relevant in industrial monitoring settings. The FDR exceeding 1 stems from the benchmark’s specific metric definition that does not normalize by reversed edges. When bounded to [0, 1], DMCD achieves an FDR of <math id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.869\pm 0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.869</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.869\pm 0.03</annotation></semantics></math>, which is on par with the other algorithms.</p>
</div>
